; host/refs.bl — the pinned HOST MODULE for `Elixir.BeamLisp.Refs`.
;
; `BeamLisp.Refs` is beam-lisp's Clojure-shaped reference runtime: atoms
; (`deref`/`swap!`/`reset!`/`compare-and-set!` plus `add-watch!`/`remove-watch!`),
; volatiles (an unsynchronized box), promises (a single-assignment cell),
; futures (a running `Task`), and the `deref` that reads all of them — including
; the two value-shaped references that live ELSEWHERE in the pinned floor,
; `BeamLisp.Delay` and `BeamLisp.Derived`.
;
; This is the `.bl` port of `lib/beam_lisp/refs.ex`'s `BeamLisp.Refs` half,
; compiled by `BeamLisp.AOT/compile_host_file` into ONE exact-named beam
; (`Elixir.BeamLisp.Refs.beam`).
;
; ── EVERY REFERENCE IS A NATIVE MEMO CELL ─────────────────────────────────
;
; An atom's value and its watch table are each ONE GC-owned memo cell; so are a
; volatile's value and a promise's state. Nothing here leaks by construction — a
; cell dies with its last reference, and a cycle a cell closes is the native
; collector's to reclaim (`native/lazy_memo/src/collector.rs`) — and two of the
; three carried a leak before:
;
;   * a volatile used to be a process-dictionary key nothing ever deleted: one
;     leaked key per `(volatile!)`, and every `take`/`drop`/`partition-all`
;     transducer instance builds one. It is now a cell written with the
;     unconditional `LazyMemo/put` (last writer wins — the volatile contract),
;     so it is also readable from another process, as Clojure's is; it is only
;     its WRITES that are unsynchronized.
;
;   * a promise used to be a looping process per promise, which nothing
;     stopped: every `(promise)` leaked a process, delivered or not. It is now
;     one cell holding `{:unset, waiters}` or `{:set, v}`; `deliver` publishes
;     `{:set, v}` and notifies every registered waiter IN THE SAME NATIVE CALL
;     — so a waiter registered before the publish is always told, and one that
;     reads after it sees the value — and a waiting `deref` registers with a
;     version CAS. No polling, and no process of its own.
;
; The `:pid` and `:key` FIELD NAMES are unchanged (see `priv/host/refs_structs.bl`):
; the struct types are public ABI and keep their shapes, so `Promise` still
; holds its state under `:pid` and `Volatile` its cell under `:key` — what
; changed is what those fields carry, not what they are called.
;
; ── VERSIONED TRANSITIONS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Every write is `LazyMemo/read_versioned` (the state WITH its publication
; counter) followed by `LazyMemo/exchange_version … :keep`. The comparison is an
; INTEGER, so the old full-term compare — the whole value loaded and compared on
; every `swap!` — is gone. A racing writer bumps the counter, the exchange
; answers `:retry`, and the loop re-reads and re-applies: Clojure's atom
; contract, and the reason a step fn must be pure.
;
; `:keep` is the edge policy for a transition that reaches nothing new — an atom
; swap, a watch-table update, a waiter registration — so it does no dependency
; walk, takes no graph lock and runs no cycle check. That is also what lets an
; atom hold a cycle the way Clojure's does: `(reset! a {:me a})` is ACCEPTED, and
; the native collector reclaims it once the atom itself is unreachable. An atom
; owns ordinary data; a cycle in it is not a reason to refuse the write.
;
; `compare-and-set!` keeps Clojure's VALUE equality (`==`: `1` and `1.0` are the
; same value) checked in the caller against the read term, while the exact race
; is still closed by the version.
;
; ── THE FIVE STRUCT TYPES (and why this file needs none of them loaded) ────
;
; `refs.ex` declares six modules, but only ONE of them carries behaviour. The
; other five are bare `defstruct` types — `Atom`, `Volatile`, `Reduced`,
; `Promise`, `Future` — pinned from ONE source (`priv/host/refs_structs.bl`, the
; `reader_exception.bl` idiom: one file, five exact module atoms, each seed entry
; carrying the `:struct-fields` that name ITS shape).
;
; A struct is only ever a TAGGED MAP: the tag is the
; `:"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom"` atom, and nothing needs `BeamLisp.Atom` to be
; LOADED to build or read one. So — exactly as `priv/host/reactive.bl` spells
; `:"Elixir.BeamLisp.Derived"` and `priv/host/deferred.bl` spells
; `:"Elixir.BeamLisp.Delay"` — every type tag here is spelled LITERALLY and
; every value is built with `maps/put` over `(maps/new)`. No `%Mod{…}` literal is
; used, so nothing is required of the compiler's struct knowledge either, and
; the five type beams can be pinned in any order relative to this one.
;
; ── WHY EVERY READ IS `erlang/map_get`, NEVER `(:field x)` ─────────────────
;
; beam-lisp's `get`/`seq`/`keys` route through the runtime's collection access,
; and `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt` classifies `BeamLisp.Atom`, `Volatile`, `Promise`,
; `Future`, `Delay` and `Derived` as REFERENCE-CELL kinds — for those, a
; collection read is REFUSED (`get: BeamLisp.Atom is a reference, not a
; collection`). The recalled Elixir module pattern-matched and used
; `atom.cell`, which is a raw map read; so is `erlang/map_get/2`. `tag/1` is the
; one guarded read: a non-map has no tag, and `maps/get/3` would raise on one.
;
; ── WHAT THE PINNED PORT OWES ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ABI parity is the contract: the same answers, the same VALUE shapes (a real
; `%BeamLisp.Atom{}`-tagged map, a real `Task` in a future), the same exception
; CLASSES AND MESSAGES, and the same PROCESS SEMANTICS —
;
;   * `swap!`/`reset!`/`compare-and-set!` run the caller's fn IN THE CALLER's
;     process and notify watches IN THE CALLER's process, after the commit;
;   * `future_exec` runs the thunk in a `Task` that INHERITS the spawning env;
;   * `promise/0` is a cell, and `deref` registers as a waiter (no polling);
;   * `deref` of a `Delay`/`Derived` delegates to the pinned `Deferred`/
;     `Reactive` modules, so the already-seeded native pair answers here.
;
; `deref` reads a Delay/Derived through the PUBLIC tag, never through the
; struct module: `(BeamLisp.Deferred/force x)` and `(BeamLisp.Reactive/deref x)`
; are calls to modules that ARE on the floor (they are seeded pairs), and they
; read the same tagged maps this file would.
;
; ── THE ONE EXPORT SUPERSET ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; A pinned module has NO PRIVATE SURFACE: the emitter exports every top-level
; `defn`, and `defn-` is not privatized on this path (the same divergence
; `priv/host/vector.bl` documents). `refs.ex`'s private helpers —
; `update_watches/2`, `notify_watches/3`, `apply_heap_bound/0` and its promise
; waiter loop — ride along as exports here, along with `tag/1`, `rethrow/1`,
; `timeout-exit?/1` and `promise_wait/2`. Every export the live module serves,
; this beam serves; the beam is a SUPERSET by exactly those. Nothing calls them
; from outside; they are the shared internals written once rather than
; duplicated per call site.
;
; ── BOOT SAFETY: NO HOST-NAMESPACE VAR IN THE PATH ────────────────────────
;
; `str` and `cons` are RT PRIMS — host-namespace vars — so a message is joined
; with the raw `erlang/iolist_to_binary` (every piece is already a binary: the
; literals and `Kernel/inspect`'s answer) and a list is grown with `erlang/++`.
; That is the discipline `priv/host/atom_guard.bl` documents ("It is NOT `str`"):
; a pinned beam must not resolve through `BeamLisp.Env` for something it can ask
; the runtime for. Field reads are `erlang/map_get`, never `(:field x)` — a
; keyword read is a COLLECTION read, which RT refuses on a reference-typed
; struct. Everything else here is a remote call to `:erlang`/`:maps`/an Elixir
; module, a guard, a language form, or an in-file helper in call position.
;
; ── THE ONE PLACE THE PORT CANNOT BE LITERAL ──────────────────────────────
;
; `deref/3` on a future is `try Task.await(task, ms) catch :exit, {:timeout, _}
; -> timeout_val`. beam-lisp's `catch` has no class-typed form (it binds one
; normalized value: `{:exit, reason}` for an exit), and its `:erlang.raise/3`
; is the raw primop that segfaults on a frame list — so the caught exit is
; re-raised by CLASS from the normalized value (`:exit` → `erlang/exit`,
; `:throw` → `throw`, anything else as an error). Class and reason are
; preserved exactly; the stack is the re-raise's own, which is all a caller can
; observe anyway (the live catch swallows the timeout's stack too). Nothing else
; in this file needs a class-typed catch.
;
; Run: bin/bl test test/bl/host_refs_test.bl test/bl/ref_cycles_test.bl

(ns host.refs)

; ── small internals ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Not private — see "the one export superset" above.

(defn- tag
  "The `:__struct__` tag of a map, else nil. Read with `maps/get/3` and guarded
   by `is_map`, never `(:__struct__ x)`: beam-lisp's keyword lookup is a
   COLLECTION read, and RT refuses one on a reference-typed struct."
  [x]
  (if (erlang/is_map x) (maps/get :__struct__ x nil) nil))

(defn- update_watches
  "CAS-loop on a watches cell, so concurrent add/remove do not lose each
   other. `f` maps the old table to the new one. `:keep` edges: a watch table
   reaches nothing new, so the exchange does no dependency walk."
  [cell f]
  (let [cur (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned cell)
        version (erlang/element 1 cur)
        old (erlang/element 2 cur)
        outcome (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version cell version (f old) :keep)]
    (cond
      (erlang/== :ok outcome) :ok
      (erlang/== :retry outcome) (update_watches cell f)
      :else
      (erlang/error
        (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception "watch table would form a reference cycle")))))

(defn- notify_watches
  "Call every watch as `(f key ref old new)`, in the CALLER's process, after the
   value has committed. A no-op change still notifies — the watch decides what
   counts as a change, not the atom (Clojure's contract)."
  [x old new]
  (let [watches (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read (erlang/map_get :watches x))]
    (if (erlang/== 0 (erlang/map_size watches))
      :ok
      (Enum/each watches
        (fn [pair]
          (BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/invoke (erlang/element 2 pair)
            (list (erlang/element 1 pair) x old new)))))))

(defn- apply_heap_bound
  "Set this process's `:max_heap_size` to the caller's env bound, if it has one.
   OTP 29 treats `kill: false` as a soft warning, not a resource bound.
   Enforce the hard limit; `future_exec` unlinks bounded tasks before they can
   allocate, so a heap kill cannot take the caller down through the Task link."
  []
  (let [words (BeamLisp.Env/max_heap)]
    (if (erlang/== nil words)
      :ok
      (erlang/process_flag :max_heap_size
        {:size words :kill true :error_logger false}))))

(defn- rethrow
  "Re-raise a value a beam-lisp `catch` bound. beam-lisp normalizes what it
   catches (`{:exit, reason}` for an exit, `{:throw, value}` for a throw, the
   normalized exception struct for an error), and has no class-typed catch — so
   the class is recovered here and raised again. See the header."
  [e]
  (if (erlang/is_map e)
    (erlang/error e)
    (if (and (erlang/is_tuple e) (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size e)))
      (cond
        (erlang/== :exit (erlang/element 1 e)) (erlang/exit (erlang/element 2 e))
        (erlang/== :throw (erlang/element 1 e)) (throw (erlang/element 2 e))
        :else (erlang/error e))
      (erlang/error e))))

(defn- timeout-exit?
  "A timed deref returns its default on timeout or a hard heap-limit kill.
   Task.await exits with `{:timeout, _}` or `{:killed, _}` respectively;
   unrelated task failures still propagate."
  [e]
  (and (erlang/is_tuple e)
       (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size e))
       (erlang/== :exit (erlang/element 1 e))
       (erlang/is_tuple (erlang/element 2 e))
       (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size (erlang/element 2 e)))
       (or (erlang/== :timeout (erlang/element 1 (erlang/element 2 e)))
           (erlang/== :killed (erlang/element 1 (erlang/element 2 e))))))

(defn- promise_wait
  "Register THIS process as a waiter on a promise cell, or answer the cell's
   `{:set, v}` state when it is already delivered. A version CAS on the native
   cell: a `deliver` that lands between the read and the exchange answers
   `:retry` and we re-read, so a waiter registered before the publish is always
   notified (`deliver` notifies in the same native call that publishes, and
   replies carry the cell so a timed-out waiter can never confuse one promise's
   value with another's). Answers `:waiting` when this process is parked."
  [cell me]
  (let [cur (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned cell)
        version (erlang/element 1 cur)
        state (erlang/element 2 cur)]
    (if (erlang/== :set (erlang/element 1 state))
      state
      (let [outcome
            (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version cell version
              (tuple :unset (erlang/++ (list me) (erlang/element 2 state))) :keep)]
        (if (erlang/== :ok outcome) :waiting (promise_wait cell me))))))

; ── atoms ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn atom
  "A new atom holding `v`: one GC-owned cell for the value, a SECOND cell for
   the watch table, and the `BeamLisp.Atom` tag. Two cells, not one map, so a
   watch table is never mistaken for part of the value."
  [v]
  (maps/put :__struct__ :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom"
    (maps/put :cell (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/create_ref v)
      (maps/put :watches (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/create_ref (maps/new)) (maps/new)))))

(defn swap!
  "Swap the atom's value: `new = (f old & args)`, published with a version
   compare-exchange retry loop, and watches notified after the commit IN THE
   CALLER's process. Returns `new`."
  ([ref f] (swap! ref f (list)))
  ([x f args]
   (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
     (let [cell (erlang/map_get :cell x)
           cur (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned cell)
           version (erlang/element 1 cur)
           old (erlang/element 2 cur)
           new (BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/invoke f (erlang/++ (list old) args))
           outcome (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version cell version new :keep)]
       (cond
         (erlang/== :ok outcome)
         (do (notify_watches x old new) new)

         ; A concurrent writer bumped the version: re-read and re-apply —
         ; Clojure's atom contract, and the reason the step fn must be pure.
         (erlang/== :retry outcome)
         (swap! x f args)

         :else
         (erlang/error
           (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
             "swap!: the new value would form a reference cycle"))))
     (erlang/error
       (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
         (list (tuple :message (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "swap!: not a reference: " (Kernel/inspect x))))))))))

(defn reset!
  "Set the atom's value to `v` (version compare-exchange retry loop),
   notifying watches after the commit. Returns `v`."
  [x v]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
    (let [cell (erlang/map_get :cell x)
          cur (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned cell)
          version (erlang/element 1 cur)
          old (erlang/element 2 cur)
          outcome (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version cell version v :keep)]
      (cond
        (erlang/== :ok outcome) (do (notify_watches x old v) v)
        (erlang/== :retry outcome) (reset! x v)
        :else
        (erlang/error
          (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
            "reset!: the value would form a reference cycle"))))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "reset!: not an atom: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))

(defn add_watch!
  "Register `f` under `key`; it is called `(f key ref old new)` on change.
   Returns the atom."
  [x key f]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
    (do (update_watches (erlang/map_get :watches x) (fn [m] (maps/put key f m))) x)
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
        (list (tuple :message (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "add-watch!: not an atom: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))))

(defn remove_watch!
  "Remove the watch registered under `key`. Returns the atom."
  [x key]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
    (do (update_watches (erlang/map_get :watches x) (fn [m] (maps/remove key m))) x)
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
        (list (tuple :message (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "remove-watch!: not an atom: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))))

(defn compare_and_set!
  "Atomically set to `new` iff the current value is `old` (VALUE equality —
   Clojure `=`, which is beam-lisp `==`, so `1` and `1.0` count as equal).
   Returns `true` on commit (`false` when the current value differs); notifies
   watches on commit."
  [x old new]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
    (let [cell (erlang/map_get :cell x)
          cur (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned cell)
          version (erlang/element 1 cur)
          current (erlang/element 2 cur)]
      ; Check value equality against the current term, then exchange against
      ; THAT read's version: a racing writer bumps the counter, the exact
      ; exchange answers `:retry`, and we re-check.
      (if (erlang/== current old)
        (let [outcome (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version cell version new :keep)]
          (cond
            (erlang/== :ok outcome) (do (notify_watches x current new) true)
            (erlang/== :retry outcome) (compare_and_set! x old new)
            :else
            (erlang/error
              (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
                "compare-and-set!: the value would form a reference cycle"))))
        false))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
        (list (tuple :message (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "compare-and-set!: not an atom: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))))

; ── volatiles ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; An unsynchronized box: ONE GC-owned ref cell, written with an unconditional
; `put` (no CAS — last writer wins, the volatile contract), so a cycle it closes
; is the collector's to reclaim. It used to be a process-dictionary key nothing
; ever deleted, which no reader outside the creating process could see and
; nothing ever reclaimed; a cell is freed with its last reference, and being a
; cell, the box IS shared — another process reads the same value. Only its
; writes are unsynchronized, which is the deal Clojure's volatiles make with
; stateful transducers (single-threaded by contract).

(defn volatile
  "A new volatile holding `v`: one ref cell."
  [v]
  (maps/put :__struct__ :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile"
    (maps/put :key (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/create_ref v) (maps/new))))

(defn vreset!
  "Overwrite the volatile's value. Returns `v`."
  [x v]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (tag x))
    (do (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/put (erlang/map_get :key x) v) v)
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "vreset!: not a volatile: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))

(defn vswap!
  "Read-modify-write the volatile. NOT atomic — there is no compare-exchange on
   this path, so a racing writer may be lost, which is exactly the volatile
   contract."
  ([vol f] (vswap! vol f (list)))
  ([x f args]
   (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (tag x))
     (let [cell (erlang/map_get :key x)
           new (BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/invoke f
                 (erlang/++ (list (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read cell)) args))]
       (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/put cell new)
       new)
     (erlang/error
       (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "vswap!: not a volatile: " (Kernel/inspect x))))))))

(defn volatile?
  "True for a volatile — a map tagged with the `BeamLisp.Volatile` struct."
  [x]
  (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (tag x)))

; ── futures ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- await_owned_start
  "A start barrier closes the publication gap: the parent acquires before
   spawning, transfers under Env's module fence, then publishes the handle.
   Parent death before publication cannot leave an orphan waiting forever."
  [parent token thunk_fn]
  (let [ref (erlang/monitor :process parent)]
    (receive
      [:bl_owned_start handle]
      (do (erlang/demonitor ref (list :flush))
          (try
            (BeamLisp.Env/bind token)
            (apply_heap_bound)
            (thunk_fn)
            (finally (BeamLisp.Env/release_code! handle))))
      [:DOWN observed :process _ _]
      (if (erlang/== ref observed) :cancelled
        (erlang/error :unexpected_start_monitor)))))

(defn- publish_owned_start [handle pid]
  (let [accepted (BeamLisp.Env/transfer_code! handle pid)]
    (if (erlang/== :cancelled accepted)
      (do (erlang/exit pid :kill) :cancelled)
      (do (erlang/send pid (tuple :bl_owned_start accepted)) :ok))))

(defn future_exec
  "An env-conveying Task with executable ownership acquired before spawning.
   Task link/cancellation semantics remain unchanged. Accepted execution holds
   the thunk and its complete managed graph until completion across reload."
  [thunk_fn]
  (let [token (BeamLisp.Env/capture) parent (erlang/self)
        generation (Process/get :bl_generation)
        graph_modules (if (erlang/== nil generation) (list)
                        (BeamLisp.Vector/to_list (erlang/map_get :modules generation)))
        handle (BeamLisp.Env/retain_code!
                 (erlang/++ graph_modules
                   (list (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info thunk_fn :module))
                         (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))))
        bounded? (erlang/not (erlang/== nil (BeamLisp.Env/max_heap)))]
    (try
      (let [task (Task/async (fn [] (await_owned_start parent token thunk_fn)))]
        (when bounded? (Process/unlink (erlang/map_get :pid task)))
        (publish_owned_start handle (erlang/map_get :pid task))
        (maps/put :__struct__ :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Future"
          (maps/put :task task (maps/new))))
      (finally (BeamLisp.Env/release_code! handle)))))

(defn spawn_owned
  "Env-conveying process with explicit extra physical module dependencies.
   Runtime boundaries retaining another callback list that callback's module
   here; managed tasks automatically retain their complete sealed graph.
   Outside managed graphs, arbitrary values hidden in closures are not traced."
  [thunk_fn kind modules]
  (let [token (BeamLisp.Env/capture) parent (erlang/self)
        generation (Process/get :bl_generation)
        graph_modules (if (erlang/== nil generation) (list)
                        (BeamLisp.Vector/to_list (erlang/map_get :modules generation)))
        modules (erlang/++ graph_modules
                  (erlang/++ modules
                    (list (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info thunk_fn :module))
                          (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))))
        handle (BeamLisp.Env/retain_code! modules)
        wrapped (fn [] (await_owned_start parent token thunk_fn))]
    (try
      (let [result (cond
                     (erlang/== :plain kind) (erlang/spawn wrapped)
                     (erlang/== :link kind) (erlang/spawn_link wrapped)
                     (erlang/== :monitor kind) (erlang/spawn_monitor wrapped)
                     :else (erlang/error (Elixir.CaseClauseError/exception (list (tuple :term kind)))))
            pid (if (erlang/== :monitor kind) (erlang/element 1 result) result)]
        (publish_owned_start handle pid)
        result)
      (finally (BeamLisp.Env/release_code! handle)))))

(defn spawn_exec
  "Spawn with env conveyance and automatic executable lifetime transfer.
   Plain accepted children survive caller death; linked children retain OTP's
   link semantics. Monitor returns the same {pid, ref} shape as Erlang."
  ([thunk_fn] (spawn_exec thunk_fn :plain))
  ([thunk_fn kind] (spawn_owned thunk_fn kind (list))))

(defn future?
  "True for a future — a map tagged with the `BeamLisp.Future` struct."
  [x]
  (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Future" (tag x)))

(defn future_cancel
  "Cancel a running future with `:brutal_kill`. Answers `true` only when it
   actually cancelled a running computation — `Task.shutdown` returns `nil` (no
   reply) for a task that had already finished."
  [x]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Future" (tag x))
    (let [task (erlang/map_get :task x)
          running (Process/alive? (erlang/map_get :pid task))]
      (Task/shutdown task :brutal_kill)
      running)
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
        (list (tuple :message (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "future-cancel: not a future: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))))

; ── promises ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; A single-assignment reference: ONE GC-owned ref cell holding `{:unset,
; waiters}` or `{:set, v}`, under the struct's `:pid` field. `deliver` publishes
; `{:set, v}` and sends `{:bl_promise, cell, v}` to every registered waiter in
; the SAME native call, so a waiter registered before the publish is always
; told and one that reads after it sees the value; a waiting `deref` parks on
; that message, keyed by the cell so a timed-out derer can never confuse one
; promise's value with another's. A waiter that times out leaves its pid in the
; list, and the delivery's one stray message is the whole cost of that — the
; cell and the promise are still reclaimed with their last reference (the old
; design left a process behind for every promise ever built).

(defn promise
  "A fresh, undelivered single-assignment reference: one cell, no process."
  []
  (maps/put :__struct__ :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Promise"
    (maps/put :pid (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/create_ref (tuple :unset (list))) (maps/new))))

(defn deliver
  "Deliver `v` to a promise. Returns the promise on the FIRST delivery, `nil` on
   any later one — Clojure allows a single delivery, and the `nil` return lets
   callers detect the no-op."
  [x v]
  (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Promise" (tag x))
    (let [cell (erlang/map_get :pid x)
          cur (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned cell)
          state (erlang/element 2 cur)]
      (if (erlang/== :set (erlang/element 1 state))
        nil
        (let [note (tuple :bl_promise cell v)
              waiters (erlang/element 2 state)
              outcome (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version
                        cell (erlang/element 1 cur) (tuple :set v) :keep
                        (Enum/map waiters (fn [w] (tuple w note))))]
          (if (erlang/== :ok outcome) x (deliver x v)))))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "deliver: not a promise: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))

; ── deref ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn deref
  "Read a derefable reference. Atoms, volatiles and reduceds read immediately;
   a future blocks until its task returns (Clojure's contract); a promise blocks
   until it is delivered; a DELAY forces it once and a DERIVED reads it current,
   so `@d` works on every one of them alike.
   `deref/3` bounds the two BLOCKING cases (future, promise) with a timeout and
   a value to answer instead; the immediate cases ignore both — exactly the
   clause set the recalled module exposed."
  ([x]
   (cond
     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
     (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read (erlang/map_get :cell x))

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Future" (tag x))
     (Task/await (erlang/map_get :task x) :infinity)

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Promise" (tag x))
     (deref x :infinity nil)

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (tag x))
     (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read (erlang/map_get :key x))

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Reduced" (tag x))
     (erlang/map_get :value x)

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Delay" (tag x))
     (BeamLisp.Deferred/force x)

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Derived" (tag x))
     (BeamLisp.Reactive/deref x)

     :else
     (erlang/error
       (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
         (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "deref: not a derefable reference: " (Kernel/inspect x)))))))

  ([x timeout_ms timeout_val]
   (cond
     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" (tag x))
     (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read (erlang/map_get :cell x))

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Future" (tag x))
     (try
       (Task/await (erlang/map_get :task x) timeout_ms)
       (catch e
         (if (timeout-exit? e) timeout_val (rethrow e))))

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile" (tag x))
     (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read (erlang/map_get :key x))

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Reduced" (tag x))
     (erlang/map_get :value x)

     (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Promise" (tag x))
     (let [cell (erlang/map_get :pid x)
           state (promise_wait cell (erlang/self))]
       (if (erlang/== :waiting state)
         (receive
           [:bl_promise c v] :when (== c cell) v
           (after timeout_ms timeout_val))
         (erlang/element 2 state)))

     ; NOTE: deliberately no Delay/Derived clause here — the recalled `deref/3`
     ; had none either, so `(deref d 10 :tv)` on a delay is refused, not a
     ; silent force.
     :else
     (erlang/error
       (Elixir.ArgumentError/exception
         (erlang/iolist_to_binary (list "deref: not a derefable reference: " (Kernel/inspect x))))))))
